    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_0",
      "quote": "evidence for a volume-­ outcome relationship has been accumulated for various medical interventions including surgeries, dialysis, perinatal as well as intensive care. Still, criticism remains. First, some studies have raised doubts about the underlying proposed volume-­ outcome relationship. Furthermore, uniform international guidance for minimum volume standards is seldom established, resulting, for example, in different cut-­ off thresholds and different regulatory approaches across different countries.",
      "reason": "The text describes the current state of knowledge (accumulated evidence for volume-outcome relationship) and important uncertainties (criticism, lack of uniform guidance)."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e2",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_0",
      "quote": "At the same time, there is no reported systematic review collating evidence on the effects of minimum volume standards in hospitals. Hence, despite the ongoing debate about the advantages and disadvantages of minimum volume standards, there has been no systematic review on the effects of minimum volume standards in hospitals. This systematic review aims to address this research gap.",
      "reason": "The text explains the importance and need for the review by highlighting the lack of existing systematic reviews on the effects of minimum volume standards and stating the aim to address this gap."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e3",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_0",
      "quote": "In Germany, a rapid review was published in 2012 by the Institute for Quality and Efficiency in Healthcare (IQWiG). In total, 10 studies were included in this report... the authors' conclusion was that 'all studies showed a design-­ related potential for bias as well as methodological shortcomings that limited the validity of the results'.",
      "reason": "The text identifies a previous systematic review (IQWiG 2012) and explains the need for the current review by citing methodological shortcomings in the previous one."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e4",
      "status": "MISSING",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "The text describes the volume-outcome relationship as the basis for minimum volume standards but does not explicitly describe the proposed mechanism of action of the intervention itself."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e5",
      "status": "MISSING",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "No logic model is mentioned or described in the provided text to show hypothesised relationships between intervention components and outcomes."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 90
}

